# Untitled

## Selective Catalytic Chemistry at Rhodium(II) Nodes in Bimetallic Metal–Organic Frameworks
The use of crystalline metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as heterogeneous catalysts provides the unique opportunity to tailor active site geometries and ensemble sizes on the atomic scale. MOFs are crystalline hybrid inorganic/organic materials with structures consisting of metal nodes of specific
geometries connected by organic linkers. Furthermore, the high porosity (>1000 m\(^{2}\) g\(^{-1}\) surface area)[1] of MOFs provides a sufficient number of highly dispersed active sites for catalytic reactions.
MOFs are reported to be excellent catalysts for a number of reactions in the liquid phase, including hydrogenation, oxidation, and epoxidation.[2] However, gas-phase heterogeneous catalysis is preferred over reactions in the liquid phase due to the ease of separating the catalyst from the products in the former case. Given their extraordinarily high surface areas, MOFs have been used as supports for metal particles in gas phase reactions.[3] Similarly, catalytically active metal complexes or clusters have been coordinated to the MOF nodes or linkers by postsynthetic modification.[4] In comparison to these catalysts that employ the MOF as a support, the use of metal nodes intrinsic to the MOF structure as the catalytically active sites has several advantages. These include: a homogeneous distribution of the metal nodes within the framework; and a well-defined structure of the metal nodes, which can be fully characterized by crystallography and can be investigated theoretically to predict the possible mechanisms of catalytic transformations.
In this work, we present the first study of gas-phase catalytic activity occurring at the metal nodes of a crystalline MOF and investigate possible reaction mechanisms using density functional theory (DFT) calculations. Specifically, (Cu\(_x\)Rh\(_{1-x}\))\(_3\)(BTC)\(_2\) (abbreviated CuRhBTC, where BTC\(^{3-}\) = benzenetricarboxylate) is found to catalyze propylene hydrogenation to propane with relatively high activity and excellent long-term stability, whereas the other bimetallic and monometallic MOFs investigated exhibit no activity. CuRhBTC is prepared via solid-state metathesis using Cu\(_3\)(BTC)\(_2\) (HKUST-1) as a precursor. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report of a +2 oxidation state for isolated Rh ions in a MOF; these Rh\(^{2+}\) ions are not stabilized by the presence of Rh–Rh bonds at Rh dimer nodes,[5] and are produced through the reduction of postsynthetically incorporated Rh\(^{3+}\). This unusual Rh\(^{2+}\) oxidation state provides the active sites for the reaction, given that activity increases with Rh concentration. DFT studies were used to model the active Rh\(^{2+}\) sites within the CuRhBTC structure and to identify an energetically favorable reaction mechanism in which H\(_2\) dissociation and propylene adsorption occur at Rh\(^{2+}\) sites.
Propylene hydrogenation experiments were carried out on the monometallic CuBTC and NiBTC MOFs, as well as a variety of Cu-containing bimetallic MOFs ((Cu\(_x\)M\(_{1-x}\))\(_3\)-
\((\mathrm{BTC})_{2})\) where \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{R}\mathbf{h}\) , Co, Ni, In and \(\mathrm{Ru}\) , as shown in Figure 1a. Only the CuRhBTC exhibited significant activity for propane production at \(20\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) \((\approx\!1600\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\) cata\(\mathrm{catalyst^{-1}m i n^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{CuRh}(33\%)\mathrm{BTC})\) . Specifically, the pure CuBTC MOF showed no activity under the same reaction conditions, indicating that the \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) ions are not active for propylene hydrogenation. Pure NiBTC also had no appreciable hydrogenation activity. Note that monometallic RhBTC was not studied due to its loss of crystallinity upon exposure to air, and the instability of RhBTC is attributed to the fact that the paddle-wheel configuration of the metal nodes is highly strained for Rh ions.[6] For all MOF catalysts, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data were collected immediately after reaction, and catalysts exhibited the same high crystallinity observed before reaction.
The activity of the \(\mathrm{CuRh}(33\%)\mathrm{BTC}\) catalyst is also stable over a 24 hour period on stream (Figure 1b). Moreover, CuRhBTC with a lower bulk concentration of Rh \((18\,\%)\) has \(45\,\%\) of the activity of the \(\mathrm{CuRh}(33\%)\mathrm{BTC}\) , which indicates that activity scales with Rh concentration and suggests that the Rh ions are the active sites for reaction. At \(50\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) , the reaction rate for \(\mathrm{CuRh}(18\%)\mathrm{BTC}\) is 3.2 times higher than at \(20\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) and is stable over the entire period on stream. The apparent activation energy for propylene hydrogenation on CuRhBTC was determined to be \(6.3\;\mathrm{kcal\,mol}^{-1}\) based on data collected at \(20{-}50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S1 in the Supporting Information).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) experiments address the oxidation state of the Rh ions in CuRhBTC (Figure 2). For the as-prepared sample, the \(\mathrm{Rh}(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})\) peak has a binding energy of \(309.2\,\mathrm{eV}.\) The spectrum of dirhodium (II) tetraacetate \((\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4})\) provides a standard for the relatively uncommon \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) oxidation state, which appears at \(308.9\,\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) and is similar to the value observed for CuRhBTC. Furthermore, the Rh(3d) spectra for highly dispersed \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) ions prepared from incipient wetness impregnation of \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) on silica shows identical binding energies and peak widths to that of CuRhBTC; therefore the relative broadness of the \(\mathrm{Rh}(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})\) peak for CuRhBTC cannot be attributed to mixed oxidation states since \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2^{-}}\) \((\mathrm{OAc})_{3}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) is equally broad and contains only \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) . In contrast, \(\mathrm{Rh}^{3+}\) in \(\mathrm{RhCl}_{3}\) appears at \(310.1\,\mathrm{eV}\) , which is
consistent with \(\mathrm{Rh}^{3+}\) oxidation states reported in the literature,[7] whereas \(\mathrm{Rh}^{1+}\) is expected at \(308.0{-}308.4\,\mathrm{eV}\) .[8,9] There is no evidence for metallic Rh at \(307.0{-}307.4\,\mathrm{eV}\) in the spectrum of CuRhBTC.[7,9] In addition, the \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) ions in CuRhBTC are stable under reducing conditions since exposure of CuRhBTC to flowing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at room temperature and \(50\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) results in almost no change in binding energy. The nearly identical Rh(3d) peak shapes for the as-prepared MOF and the post-reaction catalyst used for the hydrogenation experiment in Figure 1 are also consistent with a stable \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) oxidation state during reaction.
The X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) data provide further evidence for the assignment of the \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) oxidation state in CuRhBTC. As shown in Figure 3, the Rh
K-edge is nearly identical for CuRhBTC and the \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\), which has an oxidation state of \(+2\). In contrast, the Rh K-edges for \(\mathrm{RhCl}_{3}\) and \([\mathrm{Rh}^{1}(\mathrm{CO})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}]_{2}\) have distinctly different XANES features compared to those in the CuRhBTC spectrum (Figure S2a). The Cu K-edge for CuRhBTC is identical to that for CuBTC; a comparison with the spectrum of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}\) oxide indicates that the Cu oxidation state in CuRhBTC is consistent with \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), whereas the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}\) oxide and metallic \(\mathrm{Cu}^{0}\) standards exhibit different spectra (Figure S2b). EXAFS data were also collected and analyzed for the Cu and Rh K-edges to provide complementary information to the XANES experiments (see Supporting Information).
Ambient pressure (AP)-XPS studies establish that the Rh oxidation state in CuRhBTC does not change during exposure to the reactant gases or pure \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). Figure 4 shows the
Rh(3d) region for the CuRhBTC and this same sample: after activation by heating at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 1.5 hours in ultrahigh vacuum (UHV); during exposure to 32 mTorr \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/8\) mTorr propylene at room temperature; and in UHV following the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) propylene exposure. The Rh(3d) spectrum collected during exposure to 20 mTorr \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is also shown for another CuRhBTC sample that was initially activated at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in UHV. In all cases, the Rh(3d) spectra exhibit no appreciable change in peak shape or binding energy, implying that the Rh oxidation state remains \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) under all conditions, and this behavior is also consistent with the Rh(3d) spectrum of the post-reaction CuRhBTC (Figure 2).
Both the XPS and AP-XPS studies demonstrate that the CuRhBTC contains \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) as the main species although \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}\) is also present (Figure S3: \(\mathrm{Cu}(2\mathrm{p})\), Cu XES and C(1s) regions). Previous studies by our group have established that reduction of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}\) in pure CuBTC is induced by the combination of X-ray irradiation and heating to remove coordinating solvent molecules.[10] The fact that the post-reaction CuRhBTC exhibits a \(\mathrm{Cu}(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2})\) peak identical to that of the
pre-reaction MOF suggests that there is no change in oxidation state of the Cu ions after propylene hydrogenation (Figure S3). Moreover, exposure of CuRhBTC to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at room temperature does not cause significant reduction of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\). Consequently, there is no direct evidence that reduction of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) plays a role in propylene hydrogenation, particularly since CuBTC, which also contains both \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), is inactive for propylene hydrogenation.
In order to understand the intrinsic activity of the \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) site, propylene hydrogenation was also studied on dirhodium(II) tetraacetate \((\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4})\); this species served as a molecular model for the pure Rh paddle-wheel node. At \(20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), crystalline \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) exhibits stable hydrogenation activity \((11000\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{cat}^{-1}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1})\), which confirms that \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) ions provide the active sites (Figure S4a). The apparent activation energy for the \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) catalyst determined from activity measurements at \(0{-}40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is \(5.4\;\mathrm{kcal}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) (Figure S4b), which is a value similar to that of CuRhBTC and suggests a common mechanism. Hydrogenation activity for \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) is consistent with reports in the literature for \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\),[11] as well as for \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) ions in amorphous coordination polymers containing dinuclear rhodium paddle-wheel units.[6,12] However, the \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) active site in \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) is unstable under reaction conditions, in contrast to the high stability of \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) in CuRhBTC. Specifically, \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) is reduced to metallic Rh after exposure to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S5), and the unstable propylene hydrogenation activity for \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S4a) is attributed to reduction of \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) to metallic Rh, as confirmed by post-reaction XPS and XRD studies.
Hydrogenation activity of the CuRhBTC cannot be attributed to the presence of residual \(\mathrm{Rh}^{3+}\) ions retained in the MOF pores after exposure to \(\mathrm{RhCl}_{3}\) during ion exchange because \(\mathrm{RhCl}_{3}\) itself has no activity for propylene hydrogenation at \(20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S6). Furthermore, \(\mathrm{Rh}^{3+}\) becomes reduced at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under hydrogenation conditions, as demonstrated by the activity data as well as XPS data for \(\mathrm{RhCl}_{3}\) exposed to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Further discussion of the \(\mathrm{Rh}^{3+}\) activity is presented in the Supporting Information.
In order to better understand propylene hydrogenation on CuRhBTC, DFT studies were carried out to investigate the reaction mechanism. All calculations were performed with the Gaussian09 program package[13] using the M06-L[14] functional and the def2-TZVPP[15] basis set (see Supporting Information). The full reaction profile for propylene hydrogenation catalyzed by the bimetallic CuRh node of CuRhBTC is shown in Figure 5. Table S1 provides relative energy differences for the intermediates and transition states with respect to the CuRh node and the reactants, as well as key bond distances that demonstrate the evolution of the reaction.
The first step in the reaction mechanism is the coordination of propylene at the open site of the \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) cation (intermediate 1) via \(\pi\)-backbonding; propylene adsorbs more strongly than \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) sites by \(\approx13\;\mathrm{kcal}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) (see Supporting Information). In intermediate 1, Rh can be considered as a hexacoordinated metal after accounting for a weak bond with the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) cation (see Supporting Information), and therefore the mechanism for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dissociation is not obvious. Several possible mechanisms were considered,
including \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dissociation on penta-coordinated \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) and direct H-transfer to propylene for the formation of a propyl radical (see Supporting Information). However, the lowest energy pathway for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dissociation involves cleavage of the \(\mathrm{Rh-O}\) bond to create a second vacant site in the coordination sphere of \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) for the binding of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). Although this step has a relatively high activation energy, the decoordinated oxygen atom facilitates \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dissociation in the next step via the formation of \(\mathrm{OH}\).[16] Furthermore, the reaction barrier for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dissociation is only \(0.5\;\mathrm{kcal\,mol}^{-1}\) (TS2) and leads to the thermodynamically more stable intermediate 3. After the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) bond is cleaved, a Rh monohydride and a hydroxyl group are formed. Next, the hydrogen atom of the Rh-hydride is transferred to propylene (TS3) to form the Rh-propyl intermediate 4. Finally, a second hydrogen atom from the hydroxyl group is transferred to the propyl radical (TS4) for the formation of the final product. The desorption of propane and reformation of the \(\mathrm{Rh-O}\) bond close the catalytic cycle. Note that the activation energy barrier for \(\mathrm{Rh-O}\) bond dissociation \((\approx\!15\;\mathrm{kcal\,mol}^{-1})\) is higher than the experimentally measured activation energy of \(6.3\;\mathrm{kcal\,mol}^{-1}\). A possible explanation for this discrepancy is that CuRhBTC serves as a pre-catalyst, where species 2 provides the active sites, and the \(\mathrm{Rh-O}\) bond is not reformed in the catalytic cycle. In that case, addition of hydrogen to adsorbed propyl would be the rate-limiting step, and the calculated barrier of \(5.8\;\mathrm{kcal\,mol}^{-1}\) agrees well with the experimental value. This hypothesis will be further discussed in a subsequent publication. Investigations were also conducted on \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) as a model system for addressing activity at monometallic \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) sites, and a similar mechanism for reaction on \(\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}\) is reported in Figure S7.
Spin density plots for the CuRh node of CuRhBTC (Figures S8 and S9) demonstrate the lack of significant overlap between the half-filled \(4\mathrm{d}_{2}\) orbital on \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) and the half-filled \(3\mathrm{d}_{\mathrm{x2-y2}}\) on \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\). These results suggest minimal electronic interaction between the two metal centers, but a more detailed investigation of the bimetallic interactions and their role in the reaction is the topic of an ongoing study.
In summary, this study represents the first report of gas-phase catalysis at the metal nodes of a crystalline MOF, as
well as the first report of \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) MOF nodes prepared via the reduction of postsynthetically incorporated \(\mathrm{Rh}^{3+}\). Propylene hydrogenation activity at room temperature is observed for CuRhBTC, whereas CuBTC itself and CuMBTC \((\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\), Ir, Ni, Ru ions) have no appreciable activity, thus illustrating the unique hydrogenation activity of \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) in the CuBTC framework. The \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) ions in the bimetallic MOF structure are stable under reaction conditions for up to 24 hours and are essential for the reaction pathways, as demonstrated by DFT calculations. The main role of the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) ions in CuRhBTC is believed to be the stabilization of the MOF framework, which prevents incorporated \(\mathrm{Rh}^{2+}\) from becoming reduced to metallic Rh under reaction conditions. This work provides fundamental understanding for the rational design of MOF catalysts, which exhibit the exceptionally high selectivity typically associated with homogeneous catalysts, but are more stable under reaction conditions than their molecular analogs.
